menu "LIME"

config LIME_MEM
	tristate "LiME - Linux Memory Extractor"
	default y
	help
	  LiME (Linux Memory Extractor) is a kernel module/driver for acquiring
	  volatile memory (RAM) from Linux and Android devices for forensic
	  analysis.

	  When built as a module (M), LiME can be loaded with parameters:
	    insmod lime.ko path=/path/to/dump format=lime

	  When built-in (Y), or when loaded as module without parameters,
	  LiME exposes a sysfs interface at /sys/kernel/lime/ with the
	  following attributes:
	    - path: Output destination (file path or tcp:port)
	    - format: Output format (raw, lime, padded)
	    - dio: Direct I/O flag (0 or 1)
	    - localhostonly: TCP localhost only (0 or 1)
	    - digest: Hash algorithm (md5, sha1, sha256, etc.)
	    - timeout: Page read timeout in ms (kernel >= 2.6.35)
	    - compress: Enable compression (kernel with ZLIB_DEFLATE)
	    - state: Current acquisition state (read-only)
	    - trigger: Write 1 to start acquisition
	    - reset: Write 1 to reset state to idle

	  Example usage when built-in:
	    echo "/tmp/memory.lime" > /sys/kernel/lime/path
	    echo "lime" > /sys/kernel/lime/format
	    echo 1 > /sys/kernel/lime/trigger

	  To compile as a module, choose M here: the module will be called lime.
	  To compile into the kernel for avoiding version/symbol issues, choose Y.

endmenu
